However, I am getting the following error:
A PexException was thrown by the PexRunner: The execution process was abruptly terminated.
The exception message indicates that an exception was thrown during Pex exploration "System.ArgumentException : Object reference not set to an instance of an object."

From looking at the exception, it appears that the object being tested is null. This is odd because in the code, the assumption is that the ArrayList is not null. I need to understand, why Pex is throwing this exception when the assumption is correct? 

A: The exception "System.ArgumentException : Object reference not set to an instance of an object" usually happens when the tested object (in your case arrList) is unexpectedly null.
Consider the following line:
AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);
You're only making the assumption true, not that the ArrayList is not null.

To resolve the issue, you might consider changing
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)

to
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract(ArrayList arrList, int x)

and manually initialize the arrList parameter in the test cases, thus ensuring it's not null.
Also, make sure that the 'Microsoft.Silverlight.PerformanceProfiler.UnitTesting' (or 'Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting') namespace is being referenced for the Assert (and other utilities).
However, be aware that it will not cover any ArrayList contract method (like AddContract), but only PUT_AddContract.

A: Assuming the array list instance is instantiated before the test run, the PexRunner may abort unexpected as it detects the precondition has been met.
In your case, the instance of ArrayList is instantiated within the code but in the Pex test this instance has to be instantiated explicitly.
You can resolve the issue by creating an initialization method for arrList:
[TestInitialize]
public void Initialize()
{
    arrList = new ArrayList();
}

This method will initialize arrList before each test is run, which could prevent PexRunner from throwing null reference exceptions.
Also, the PexRunner will not execute methods having arrList as the first argument if it is not initialized, so the test method might still not work correctly if arrList is null at the time of execution.
Make sure your Pex file is configured to cover both paths, the one with a properly initialized arrList and the one with a null arrList. Also, make sure the PexProjectSettings includes TestCategory "@Default" and the UnderTestType "@This", which correspond properly to the code.

A: The issue you are experiencing is related to the fact that you are assuming the object is not null but you are not initializing it in your test case.
You are not initializing arrList in your test case so it gets nullified which causes the Pex runner to crash with an 'Argument Null Exception'.

You can either initialize arrList in your test before using it or modify the PexMethod signature by passing a [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute, where the arrList is an instance to be tested.

Or modify the test case initialization by adding:

    [TestInitialize]
    public void Initialize()
    {
        arrList = new ArrayList();
    }

This way, the ArrayList instance arrList will--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListIndexOfX < New_arrListCount); //the returned index is within the range of the list 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that x is contained within the list after addition.
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == true); //x is indeed contained within the list after addition.
        }
    }
