And is called by
[TestFixture]
public class TestExample
{
    [Test]
    [PexMethod]
    public void TestExampleMethod([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList)
    {
        arrList.Add(PUT_AddContract(arrList, PexSettings.Default.MaxInt32));
    }
}

However, when I try to compile the above code in Visual Studio 2012, I get this error:

The call to method 'PUT_AddContract' is not made in method
  'ArrayListContractTest.TestExampleMethod'

I'm not sure how to resolve this issue.
This code was working flawlessly with Moq and Rhino.Mocks long ago, but now it seems to only work when I use 'Arrange' and 'Act' in the Pex tests as shown with PexMethod. Unfortunately, this is not appropriate in this particular scenario. Any other solutions or work arounds?

A: The issue you're encountering is due to the fact that Pex methods aren't automatically called by the PexFramework, unlike methods marked with [PexMethod] attribute.
As a solution, you can call the PUT_AddContract method inside the TestExampleMethod like below:
[TestFixture]
public class TestExample
{
    [Test]
    [PexMethod]
    public void TestExampleMethod([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList)
    {
        ArrayListContractTest testTarget = new ArrayListContractTest();
        testTarget.PUT_AddContract(arrList, PexSettings.Default.MaxInt32);
    }
}

Here we create an instance of the ArrayListContractTest class and use it to call the PUT_AddContract method. This way, PUT_AddContract would be executed in the context of the TestExampleMethod.
Please note that calling Pex methods this way may not show you desired behavior in the Pex Lens due to the lack of [PexMethod] annotations, so you may want to refactor the code to reflect this fact.
If Pex doesn't understand what to do, consider creating additional simple tests to cover edge-cases.
Also note that in order for all the assertions in PUT_AddContract to hold, you should replace the ArrayList with a type that implements the IList interface. ArrayList doesn't implement IList, and therefore adding an assertion like Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x)) will result in an exception because the ArrayList class doesn't provide a method to check if it contains a particular value.
I hope the provided solutions will do good enough for you.

A: You don't need to call the PUT_AddContract method directly from your test method. Pex tests are supposed to drive the execution of methods, and hence don't need to manually call those same methods.
What you can try is to replace PUT_AddContract method with a public method in the ArrayList class and then test this method directly.
This would look something like this:
[TestFixture]
public class TestExample
{
    [Test]
    [PexMethod]
    public void TestExampleMethod([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList)
    {
        int x = ...
        arrList.Add(x);
        PexVerify.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", arrList.Count);
        PexVerify.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", arrList.Contains(x));
    }
}

I'm assuming that you would re-implement the arrList.Add(x) by yourself using PexVerify in TestExampleMethod to cover the contract check for Add method.
And I didn't know your class structure specifically but I suggest checking out about PexContractCheck.txt that provides a step-by-step guide about how to add a contract check in your test. 
If nothing works out, ensure your references, settings are correct. Test the method--------------------            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX);
            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ ret is valid ie >= 0 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= 0);
        }
    }

